      "text": "<think>\nOkay, so I have this logical reasoning problem to solve. Let me try to break it down step by step. The context gives me three statements about George and his composing. The question is whether the statement \"If George will not compose, George cannot compose\" is true, false, or uncertain. The options are A) True, B) False, C) Uncertain.\n\nFirst, I'll list out the given statements to make it clearer:\n\n1. If George likes music, he wants to compose. (Likes \u2192 Wants)\n2. If George has access to a program, he can compose. (Access \u2192 Can)\n3. If George can compose and wants to compose, he will compose. (Can \u2227 Wants \u2192 Will)\n\nNow, the statement to evaluate is: If George will not compose, then George cannot compose. In logical terms, this is \u00acWill \u2192 \u00acCan.\n\nI need to see if this statement is necessarily true, false, or uncertain based on the given premises.\n\nLet me represent each statement symbolically:\n\n1. L \u2192 W (L is Likes, W is Wants)\n2. A \u2192 C (A is Access, C is Can)\n3. (C \u2227 W) \u2192 Will (Will is Will compose)\n\nThe statement to evaluate is \u00acWill \u2192 \u00acC.\n\nI think I should use contraposition here. The contrapositive of the third statement is \u00acWill \u2192 \u00ac(C \u2227 W). Which is equivalent to \u00acWill \u2192 (\u00acC \u2228 \u00acW). So, if George will not compose, then either he cannot compose or he does not want to compose.\n\nBut the statement we're evaluating is stronger: it says that if he won't compose, then he cannot compose. That is, \u00acWill \u2192 \u00acC. But from the contrapositive, we only know that either \u00acC or \u00acW is true. So, it's possible that George can compose (C is true) but doesn't want to (W is false), which would make \u00acWill true, but \u00acC would be false. Therefore, the statement \u00acWill \u2192 \u00acC isn't necessarily true because there's a scenario where George can compose but chooses not to, hence he won't compose