2192 \u00ac(C \u2227 W), which is \u00acWill \u2192 (\u00acC \u2228 \u00acW). So, if George won't compose, then either he can't compose or he doesn't want to. The statement in question is \u00acWill \u2192 \u00acC, which is a stronger statement than the contrapositive. Because the contrapositive allows for either \u00acC or \u00acW, but the statement in question only allows for \u00acC. Therefore, the statement in question is not necessarily true because there's a possibility that \u00acW is true instead of \u00acC. So, the statement is false because it's not always true.\n\nWait, but another way to look at it is: If George can compose (C is true), and he wants to compose (W is true), then he will compose (Will is true). So, if he won't compose (\u00acWill), then it's not the case that both C and W are true. So, either he can't compose or he doesn't want to. Therefore, the statement in question is saying that if he won't compose, then he can't compose. But it's possible that he can compose but doesn't want to, so the statement is not necessarily true. Hence, the statement is false because it's not always true.\n\nWait, but let me think about it in terms of logical equivalence. The statement in question is \u00acWill \u2192 \u00acC. The contrapositive of this is C \u2192 Will. But from the premises, we have (C \u2227 W) \u2192 Will. So, C alone doesn't necessarily lead to Will; it's C and W together. Therefore, the statement C \u2192 Will is not necessarily true because W might be false. So, the contrapositive of the statement in question (\u00acWill \u2192 \u00acC) is C \u2192 Will, which isn't necessarily true because Will could be false even if C is true, as long as W is false. Therefore, the statement in question is not necessarily true, so it's false.\n\nWait, but I'm getting a bit confused. Let me try to structure it more clearly.\n\nGiven:\n\n1. L \u2192 W\n2. A \u2192 C\n3. (C \u2227 W) \u2192 Will\n\nWe need to evaluate: \u00acWill \u2192 \u00acC.\n\nIs this