be false because the antecedent is true, and the consequent is false.\n\nScenario 3: George can't compose (C=false), wants to compose (W=true). Then, (C \u2227 W) is false, so Will could be false. In this case, \u00acWill is true, and \u00acC is true. So, the implication \u00acWill \u2192 \u00acC is true.\n\nScenario 4: George can't compose (C=false), doesn't want to compose (W=false). Then, (C \u2227 W) is false, so Will could be false. So, \u00acWill is true, and \u00acC is true. The implication is true.\n\nFrom these scenarios, in scenario 2, the implication \u00acWill \u2192 \u00acC is false. Therefore, the statement is not always true, so it's false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"If George will not compose, George cannot compose\" is false because it doesn't account for the possibility that George might not compose due to not wanting to, even if he can compose.\n\n**Answer: B) False**",
