s a student (Sj). \n\nFrom premise 1, all students are humans. So, since Jerry is a student, he is a human (Hj). So, Hj is true.\n\nNow, let's look at Rose. From premise 6, Rose is young or a student: Yr \u2228 Sr. So, either she's young, or she's a student, or both.\n\nFrom premise 4, no young person teaches. So, if Rose is young (Yr), then she doesn't teach (\u00acTr). But from premise 3, everyone either studies or teaches. So, if Rose is young, she doesn't teach, so she must study. \n\nIf Rose studies, from premise 2, she is a student (Sr). So, if Rose is young, she is a student. Alternatively, if she's not young, then from premise 6, she must be a student. So, in either case, Rose is a student. Therefore, Sr is true.\n\nSo, putting it together: Rose is a student (Sr) and Jerry is a human (Hj). Therefore, the statement Sr \u2227 Hj is true. The question is asking if \u00ac(Sr \u2227 Hj) is true, which would mean it's not true that both are the case. But since both are true, \u00ac(Sr \u2227 Hj) is false. Therefore, the statement is false, so the correct answer would be B) False.\n\nWait, let me double-check to make sure I didn't miss anything. \n\nFor Jerry: \u00acTj (doesn't teach) \u2192 from premise 3, he must study. Studying \u2192 Sj (student). Sj \u2192 Hj (human). So, Hj is definitely true.\n\nFor Rose: Yr \u2228 Sr. If Yr is true, then from premise 4, \u00acTr. From premise 3, since she doesn't teach, she must study. Studying \u2192 Sr. So, if Yr is true, Sr is true. If Yr is false, then Sr must be true. So, Sr is definitely true.\n\nTherefore, Sr \u2227 Hj is true, so \u00ac(Sr \u2227 Hj) is false. So, the statement is false, so the answer is B) False.\n</think>\n\nThe statement \"It is not true that Rose is a student and Jerry is a human\" is false because both Rose is a student and Jerry is a human are true.\n\n**Answer: B) False**",
