eeds money, then she doesn't need money. That's a contradiction, so the only way to avoid it is if Mary doesn't work or doesn't need money. But if she doesn't work, then she doesn't order takeout, so she enjoys dining halls. But does that mean she doesn't need money? Not necessarily. So, perhaps Mary needs money, but she doesn't work, which is possible. But the context doesn't provide information about other ways she might earn money.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the conclusion is that Mary doesn't need money because if she worked, she would need money, but that leads to a contradiction, so she must not need money. But I'm not sure.\n\nWait, let's try to structure this with logical implications.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nW: Mary works in student jobs on campus.\n\nN: Mary needs to earn money.\n\nT: Mary orders takeout frequently.\n\nE: Mary enjoys dining hall meals.\n\nP: Mary is picky.\n\nFrom the statements:\n\n1. W \u2192 N\n\n2. T \u2192 W\n\n3. T \u2228 E\n\n4. E \u2192 \u00acP\n\n5. E \u2192 (spends time eating and catching up)\n\n6. (W \u2227 N) \u2192 (\u00acP \u2227 \u00acN)\n\nNow, the question is about N: does Mary need money?\n\nLet's assume W is true. Then from 1, N is true. But from 6, (W \u2227 N) \u2192 \u00acN, which is a contradiction. Therefore, W must be false.\n\nSo, W is false. Therefore, from 2, \u00acW \u2192 \u00acT (contrapositive). So, T is false.\n\nFrom 3, T \u2228 E, and since T is false, E must be true.\n\nFrom E being true, from 4, \u00acP is true.\n\nBut what about N? From 1, W \u2192 N, but W is false, so N could be either true or false. The statement 1 doesn't say anything about N when W is false. So, N could be true or false.\n\nBut wait, statement 6 says that if W and N are both true, then \u00acN is true. But since W is false, the antecedent of statement 6 is false, so the implication is true regardless of N. So, statement 6 doesn't give us any information about N when W is false.\n\nTherefore, N could be true or false. So, we can't deter