ed isn't domesticated, he can't be a pet. But the context doesn't explicitly state that all pets are domesticated. It only says that some pets are cows, which are domesticated. So, perhaps there are pets that are not domesticated. However, in reality, pets are usually domesticated, but the context might not make that assumption.\n\nWait, but the context says that if something is a bovine, it's domesticated. So, any pet that is a cow is domesticated. But the context doesn't say that all pets are bovines or that all pets are domesticated. So, perhaps there are pets that are not bovines and not domesticated. But then, the context doesn't provide any information about that.\n\nHowever, the key point is that Ted is an alligator, and alligators are not domesticated. So, if being a pet requires being domesticated, then Ted can't be a pet. But if the context allows for pets to be non-domesticated, then Ted could be a pet. But the context doesn't specify that.\n\nWait, but the context says that some pets are cows, which are domesticated. It doesn't say that all pets are domesticated. So, perhaps there are pets that are not domesticated. But then, the context doesn't provide any information about whether alligators can be pets or not.\n\nWait, but the context says that no domesticated animals are alligators. So, alligators are not domesticated. Therefore, if Ted is an alligator, he's not domesticated. Now, if being a pet requires being domesticated, then Ted can't be a pet. But if the context allows for pets to be non-domesticated, then Ted could be a pet. However, the context doesn't specify that.\n\nBut in the context, the only information about pets is that some are cows, which are domesticated. It doesn't say anything about other pets. So, perhaps Ted could be a pet if pets can be non-domesticated. But that seems unlikely because, in general, pets are domesticated. However, the context doesn't explicitly state that.\n\nWait, but the context says that if something is a bov